From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000058 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: OMAP4: cpuidle: steelhead's C2/C3 enabled by default, as on stock

Patch 0024 registered C2 and C3 with CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF, so the states
existed and had counters but the governor never picked them until they
were armed by hand through sysfs. That was the right default while
every attempt hung:
- r5 and r6 armed them at boot and hung;
- r7-r11 hung whenever CPU0 went OFF on a running box.

Those hangs are understood and fixed in this series:
- 0050: CPU1's A9 errata bits through PPA 0x25, which the resume needed;
- 0051: hci_bcm releases the BT UART while idle, so the 170 us QoS stops
  vetoing C2/C3;
- 0052/0053: a pending level GPIO interrupt vetoes the entry and wakes
  CPU1, so no WiFi interrupt is lost;
- 0054-0057: stock's table, targets and PM setup.

With all of that in, both states were armed for an 8 h overnight soak
(2026-09-24): 3.67 M C3 entries per CPU, 86 % of idle time in C3, no hang,
no watchdog reset, no WiFi repair. Then for an hour of Spotify playback
(2026-09-25): 0 XRUNs, 0 underruns, 54 % of the time in C3 while playing.
Audio's own CPU-latency request (1312 us) sits above both exit latencies,
so playback does not veto them.

So register them enabled, as stock does. The first deep entries now
happen at boot again, before hci_bcm is up, which is where r5/r6 hung.
Those hangs are attributed to the missing CPU1 errata bits (0050); the
first r17 boot is the check, and it goes through the kernel-OTA trial
slot, so a hang costs a power cycle, not a reflash.

`echo 1 > .../cpuidle/state{1,2}/disable` still takes a state out at
runtime.

Signed-off-by: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
---
diff --git a/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c b/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
index 4e5775e44..bd5a33b35 100644
--- a/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-omap2/cpuidle44xx.c
@@ -113,8 +113,8 @@ static struct idle_statedata omap5_idle_data[] = {
  * default). Reproduce them, so the entry path can be taken apart step by step
  * on a booted box.
  *
- * Arming a state needs no knob here: CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF (patch 0024) sets the
- * USER disable bit, so the per-CPU sysfs `disable` file is the switch.
+ * Disarming a state needs no knob here either: the per-CPU sysfs `disable`
+ * file sets the USER disable bit, which the governor honours.
  *
  * keep_mpu_on / keep_core_on must PROGRAM the domain ON, not merely leave it
  * alone: mainline's pwrdms_setup() (pm44xx.c) arms mpu_pwrdm and core_pwrdm for
@@ -124,8 +124,8 @@ static struct idle_statedata omap5_idle_data[] = {
  * pwrdms_setup (0xc0012d04) left both ON, which is why its knobs could be
  * "skip programming" and still mean "stays ON".
  *
- * Every knob defaults to its no-op value, so an untouched boot behaves exactly
- * as before: C1 only, C2 and C3 registered but disabled.
+ * Every knob defaults to its no-op value, so an untouched boot runs stock's
+ * table unmodified: C1, C2 and C3 all selectable.
  */
 static bool skip_cpu1_wait;	/* skip the wait for CPU1 to reach OFF */
 static bool keep_mpu_on;	/* hold mpu_pwrdm ON  (stock: keep_mpu_on) */
@@ -477,39 +477,29 @@ int __init omap4_idle_init(void)
 		return -ENODEV;
 
 	/*
-	 * steelhead (HS OMAP4460): C2 and C3 drive MPUSS power transitions that
-	 * trap into the HS secure monitor. The historical failure was a CPU1
-	 * cpuidle panic with SMP online - stock 3.0.8 guarded the same paths
-	 * with its cpuidle44xx.disallow_smp_idle parameter - so for a long time
-	 * this truncated the driver to C1 (state_count = 1) and the deep states
-	 * did not exist at all.
+	 * steelhead (HS OMAP4460): C2 and C3 are ENABLED from registration, as
+	 * on stock. They drive MPUSS power transitions that trap into the HS
+	 * secure monitor, and for most of this port's life they were either
+	 * truncated away (state_count = 1) or registered with CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF
+	 * (patch 0024), because every early attempt hung: a boot with them
+	 * armed (r5, r6) and CPU0 going OFF on a running box (r7-r11).
 	 *
-	 * Register them DISABLED instead. CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF leaves a state
-	 * visible in sysfs with disable=1, so the governor never selects it and
-	 * a boot is exactly as safe as C1-only, while
+	 * What made them safe, each found on the unit and each in this series:
+	 *   0050  CPU1's A9 errata bits (PPA 0x25) -- the resume that hung;
+	 *   0051  hci_bcm releases the BT UART while idle, so its 170 us
+	 *         CPU-latency QoS no longer vetoes C2/C3;
+	 *   0052/0053  a pending level GPIO interrupt vetoes the entry, and
+	 *         CPU1 is woken on veto, so WiFi interrupts are not lost;
+	 *   0054-0057  stock's C-state table, MPU/CORE targets and PM setup.
+	 * Then: an 8 h overnight soak with both armed (2026-09-24, 3.67 M C3
+	 * entries per CPU, no hang, no WiFi repair) and an hour of playback
+	 * (2026-09-25, 0 XRUNs, 54 % of the time in C3 while playing).
 	 *
-	 *   echo 0 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpuidle/state1/disable
-	 *
-	 * (and the same for cpu1) arms one for a measured experiment.
-	 * CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF sets the USER disable bit (drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle.c),
-	 * so that sysfs file is exactly the switch. If the experiment wedges the
-	 * box, a reboot restores the safe default; nothing has to be reflashed.
-	 * Registering the states also makes their residency counters exist.
-	 *
-	 * Arming at BOOT was tried twice (r5, r6) and hung the board before the
-	 * display came up. A running box needs two more things before the
-	 * governor can pick C2 at all: the Bluetooth UART must be released (while
-	 * hci_uart holds it, 8250_omap keeps a 170 us CPU-latency QoS request at
-	 * 3 Mbaud, below C2's 768 us exit latency), and the escape hatches of
-	 * patch 0048 decide how much of the entry path runs. See
-	 * docs/2026-09-20-sleep-states-design.md sections 4m and 4n.
+	 * `echo 1 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu[01]/cpuidle/state{1,2}/disable`
+	 * still takes a state out by hand, at runtime.
+	 * docs/2026-09-20-sleep-states-design.md has the whole story.
 	 */
 	if (of_machine_is_compatible("google,steelhead")) {
-		int i;
-
-		for (i = 1; i < idle_driver->state_count; i++)
-			idle_driver->states[i].flags |= CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF;
-
 		/* stock's table and numbers, see steelhead_idle_data */
 		core_pd = pwrdm_lookup("core_pwrdm");
 		if (!core_pd)
